window.DP_INTERVIEW.questions.push(
  {q:"What is PEP 8?",level:"Python Basics",difficulty:"Beginner",experience:"0-3 Years",company:["TCS","Infosys","Cognizant"],answer:"PEP 8 is the official style guide for writing Python code. It covers indentation (4 spaces), naming conventions (snake_case for variables, PascalCase for classes), line length (79 chars), and spacing rules.",tips:"Mention tools like black, flake8, and pylint that auto-check PEP 8 compliance."},
  {q:"What is the difference between Python 2 and Python 3?",level:"Python Basics",difficulty:"Beginner",experience:"0-3 Years",company:["Wipro","Accenture","TCS"],answer:"Python 3 has print() as a function, true division with /, Unicode strings by default, and range() returns an iterator. Python 2 is end-of-life (2020) and should not be used.",tips:"Just say 'always use Python 3, Python 2 is dead' — shows you are current."},
  {q:"What are Python's built-in data types?",level:"Python Basics",difficulty:"Beginner",experience:"0-2 Years",company:["Infosys","TCS","Zoho"],answer:"Numeric (int, float, complex), Sequence (str, list, tuple, range), Mapping (dict), Set (set, frozenset), Boolean (bool), Binary (bytes, bytearray), and NoneType.",tips:"Group them into categories rather than listing randomly — shows organized thinking."},
  {q:"What is type casting? Explain implicit vs explicit.",level:"Python Basics",difficulty:"Beginner",experience:"0-2 Years",company:["TCS","Cognizant","Startups"],answer:"Type casting converts one data type to another. Implicit is automatic (Python converts int to float in 5+2.0). Explicit is manual using int(), float(), str(), etc.",code:`x = 5 + 2.0      # implicit -> float\ny = int("42")    # explicit\nprint(x, y)`,output:`7.0 42`,tips:"Give one example of each to make it concrete."},
  {q:"What does the 'pass' statement do?",level:"Python Basics",difficulty:"Beginner",experience:"0-2 Years",company:["Zoho","Freshworks"],answer:"'pass' is a null statement that does nothing. It is a placeholder used where syntax requires a statement but you don't want any action yet, like an empty function or class body.",tips:"Contrast with 'continue' (skips loop iteration) and 'break' (exits loop)."},
  {q:"What is the difference between 'is' and '=='  for strings?",level:"Python Basics",difficulty:"Intermediate",experience:"1-4 Years",company:["Amazon","Adobe"],answer:"'==' compares string values. 'is' compares identity. Due to string interning, small/simple strings may share identity, but never rely on 'is' for value comparison.",tips:"Always use == for string comparison; is only for None."},
  {q:"What are f-strings and why are they preferred?",level:"Python Basics",difficulty:"Beginner",experience:"0-3 Years",company:["Startups","Freshworks","Zoho"],answer:"f-strings (formatted string literals, Python 3.6+) let you embed expressions inside strings using {}. They are faster and more readable than % formatting or .format().",code:`name, age = "Ravi", 25\nprint(f"{name} is {age}")`,output:`Ravi is 25`,tips:"Mention you can do calculations inside: f'{a+b}'."},
  {q:"How does Python handle memory management?",level:"Python Basics",difficulty:"Advanced",experience:"3-7 Years",company:["Google","Microsoft","Amazon"],answer:"Python uses automatic memory management with reference counting plus a cyclic garbage collector. Objects are freed when their reference count hits zero. The gc module handles reference cycles.",tips:"Mention reference counting AND the cyclic garbage collector for full marks."},
  {q:"What is string slicing? Explain the syntax.",level:"Python Basics",difficulty:"Beginner",experience:"0-2 Years",company:["TCS","Infosys","Amazon"],answer:"Slicing extracts part of a string using [start:stop:step]. start is inclusive, stop is exclusive. Negative indices count from the end. s[::-1] reverses a string.",code:`s = "Python"\nprint(s[0:3], s[::-1])`,output:`Pyt nohtyP`,tips:"The reverse trick s[::-1] is asked very often — memorize it."},
  {q:"What is the difference between find() and index() for strings?",level:"Python Basics",difficulty:"Intermediate",experience:"1-3 Years",company:["Zoho","TCS"],answer:"Both search for a substring. find() returns -1 if not found (safe). index() raises a ValueError if not found. Use find() when the substring might be absent.",tips:"Say 'find is safe, index raises an error' — crisp and memorable."},
  {q:"How do you remove whitespace from a string?",level:"Python Basics",difficulty:"Beginner",experience:"0-2 Years",company:["TCS","Startups"],answer:"strip() removes leading and trailing whitespace, lstrip() removes left only, rstrip() removes right only. You can also pass characters to strip specific ones.",code:`s = "  hello  "\nprint(f"[{s.strip()}]")`,output:`[hello]`,tips:"Common in cleaning user input — mention that real-world use."},
  {q:"What is the difference between append() and extend()?",level:"Collections",difficulty:"Beginner",experience:"0-3 Years",company:["Amazon","Zoho","Infosys"],answer:"append() adds its argument as a SINGLE element (even if it's a list). extend() adds each element of an iterable individually.",code:`a=[1,2]; a.append([3,4]); print(a)\nb=[1,2]; b.extend([3,4]); print(b)`,output:`[1, 2, [3, 4]]\n[1, 2, 3, 4]`,tips:"The nested-list difference is the key insight interviewers test."},
  {q:"How do you reverse a number in Python?",level:"Python Basics",difficulty:"Beginner",experience:"0-3 Years",company:["TCS","Wipro","Zoho"],answer:"Use modulo and integer division in a loop: extract the last digit with n%10, build the reverse with rev=rev*10+digit, then remove the digit with n//=10.",code:`n=1234; rev=0\nwhile n>0:\n    rev=rev*10+n%10\n    n//=10\nprint(rev)`,output:`4321`,tips:"Also mention the shortcut: int(str(n)[::-1]) for positive numbers."},
  {q:"What is the walrus operator := ?",level:"Python Basics",difficulty:"Intermediate",experience:"2-5 Years",company:["Google","Microsoft"],answer:"The walrus operator (Python 3.8+) assigns a value to a variable as part of an expression. Useful in while loops and comprehensions to avoid repeating calculations.",code:`if (n := 10) > 5:\n    print(n)`,output:`10`,tips:"Mention it reduces redundant function calls in loops."},
  {q:"How do you swap two variables without a temp variable?",level:"Python Basics",difficulty:"Beginner",experience:"0-3 Years",company:["Zoho","Freshworks","TCS"],answer:"Python allows tuple unpacking: a, b = b, a. Python evaluates the right side first as a tuple, then assigns.",code:`a,b=5,10\na,b=b,a\nprint(a,b)`,output:`10 5`,tips:"This is a favorite fresher question — know it cold."},
  {q:"What is the difference between sort() and sorted()?",level:"Collections",difficulty:"Intermediate",experience:"1-4 Years",company:["Amazon","Adobe","Microsoft"],answer:"sort() modifies the list in-place and returns None. sorted() returns a NEW sorted list and works on any iterable, leaving the original unchanged.",code:`a=[3,1,2]\nprint(sorted(a), a)\na.sort()\nprint(a)`,output:`[1, 2, 3] [3, 1, 2]\n[1, 2, 3]`,tips:"Say 'sort mutates, sorted returns new' — clear distinction."},
  {q:"What are Python keywords? How many are there?",level:"Python Basics",difficulty:"Beginner",experience:"0-2 Years",company:["TCS","Infosys"],answer:"Keywords are reserved words with special meaning that cannot be used as identifiers. Modern Python has 35 keywords like if, else, for, while, def, class, True, False, None.",code:`import keyword\nprint(len(keyword.kwlist))`,output:`35`,tips:"Mention you can list them with keyword.kwlist."},
  {q:"What is the difference between del, remove(), and pop()?",level:"Collections",difficulty:"Intermediate",experience:"1-4 Years",company:["Amazon","Zoho","Cognizant"],answer:"del removes by index (or deletes the variable). remove() deletes the first matching VALUE. pop() removes by index AND returns the removed item (default: last).",code:`a=[10,20,30]\na.pop(); a.remove(10); print(a)`,output:`[20]`,tips:"Remember: remove by value, pop by index + returns it, del by index."},
  {q:"How do you count occurrences of an element in a list?",level:"Collections",difficulty:"Beginner",experience:"0-3 Years",company:["TCS","Zoho"],answer:"Use the list.count(x) method for one element, or collections.Counter for all elements at once.",code:`from collections import Counter\nprint(Counter([1,1,2,3,3,3]))`,output:`Counter({3: 3, 1: 2, 2: 1})`,tips:"Counter is the professional way — mention it."},
  {q:"What is string immutability and why does it matter?",level:"Python Basics",difficulty:"Intermediate",experience:"1-4 Years",company:["Microsoft","Adobe"],answer:"Strings cannot be changed after creation. Any 'modification' creates a new string. This matters for performance — building strings in a loop with += is slow; use join() instead.",code:`words=["a","b","c"]\nprint("".join(words))`,output:`abc`,tips:"Mention join() over += in loops — shows performance awareness."},
  {q:"What does the zip() function do?",level:"Python Basics",difficulty:"Beginner",experience:"1-3 Years",company:["Amazon","Zoho","Startups"],answer:"zip() combines multiple iterables element-wise into tuples. It stops at the shortest iterable. Useful for iterating over two lists in parallel.",code:`names=["a","b"]; ages=[1,2]\nprint(list(zip(names,ages)))`,output:`[('a', 1), ('b', 2)]`,tips:"Mention it's used to build dicts: dict(zip(keys, values))."},
  {q:"What is the difference between range and xrange?",level:"Control Flow",difficulty:"Beginner",experience:"0-3 Years",company:["TCS","Infosys"],answer:"In Python 3, range() is lazy (like Python 2's xrange) — it generates numbers on demand, saving memory. xrange does not exist in Python 3.",tips:"Note that xrange is Python 2 only; range in Py3 already behaves lazily."},
  {q:"How do you check the data type of a variable?",level:"Python Basics",difficulty:"Beginner",experience:"0-2 Years",company:["TCS","Startups"],answer:"Use type(x) to get the class, or isinstance(x, Type) to check if x is an instance of a type. isinstance is preferred as it supports inheritance.",code:`print(type(5), isinstance(5, int))`,output:`<class 'int'> True`,tips:"Prefer isinstance() over type() == for inheritance support."},
  {q:"What is the ternary (conditional) operator in Python?",level:"Control Flow",difficulty:"Beginner",experience:"0-3 Years",company:["Zoho","TCS","Amazon"],answer:"A one-line if-else: value_if_true if condition else value_if_false. It makes simple conditionals concise.",code:`age=20\nprint("Adult" if age>=18 else "Minor")`,output:`Adult`,tips:"Don't nest ternaries too deep — readability suffers."},
  {q:"What is the difference between % , .format() and f-strings?",level:"Python Basics",difficulty:"Intermediate",experience:"1-4 Years",company:["Adobe","Microsoft"],answer:"% is the oldest C-style formatting. .format() is more powerful and positional. f-strings (3.6+) are the newest, fastest, and most readable. Use f-strings in modern code.",tips:"Recommend f-strings but show you know all three exist."}
);


/* ==================== BATCH: FUNCTIONS, OOP, ADVANCED ==================== */
window.DP_INTERVIEW.questions.push(
  {q:"What is a lambda function?",level:"Functions",difficulty:"Beginner",experience:"0-4 Years",company:["Amazon","Zoho","Infosys"],answer:"A lambda is a small anonymous function defined with the lambda keyword. Syntax: lambda args: expression. It can have any number of arguments but only one expression.",code:`square = lambda x: x*x\nprint(square(5))`,output:`25`,tips:"Mention common use with map(), filter(), and sorted(key=...)."},
  {q:"What is the difference between a function and a method?",level:"Functions",difficulty:"Beginner",experience:"0-3 Years",company:["TCS","Cognizant"],answer:"A function is a standalone block of code called by name. A method is a function that belongs to an object/class and is called on an instance (obj.method()).",tips:"Methods automatically receive 'self' as the first argument."},
  {q:"What is a closure in Python?",level:"Functions",difficulty:"Advanced",experience:"3-7 Years",company:["Google","Meta","PayPal"],answer:"A closure is a nested function that remembers variables from its enclosing scope even after the outer function has finished. Used for data hiding and function factories.",code:`def multiplier(n):\n    def mul(x): return x*n\n    return mul\ntimes3 = multiplier(3)\nprint(times3(5))`,output:`15`,tips:"Closures are the foundation of decorators — mention that link."},
  {q:"What is recursion? What is a base case?",level:"Functions",difficulty:"Intermediate",experience:"1-5 Years",company:["Amazon","Microsoft","Zoho"],answer:"Recursion is when a function calls itself. The base case is the stopping condition that prevents infinite recursion. Without it you get a RecursionError (stack overflow).",code:`def fact(n):\n    if n<=1: return 1\n    return n*fact(n-1)\nprint(fact(5))`,output:`120`,tips:"Always state the base case first — interviewers check for it."},
  {q:"What is the difference between global and local scope?",level:"Functions",difficulty:"Intermediate",experience:"1-4 Years",company:["TCS","Adobe"],answer:"Local variables exist only inside a function. Global variables exist at module level. Use the 'global' keyword to modify a global inside a function. Python follows LEGB rule (Local, Enclosing, Global, Built-in).",tips:"Mention the LEGB rule for name resolution — impresses interviewers."},
  {q:"What are default arguments and the mutable default trap?",level:"Functions",difficulty:"Advanced",experience:"2-6 Years",company:["Google","Amazon","Netflix"],answer:"Default arguments provide fallback values. The trap: using a mutable default (like []) shares it across ALL calls because it's created once at definition time.",code:`def f(x=None):\n    x = x or []\n    x.append(1)\n    return x\nprint(f(), f())`,output:`[1] [1]`,tips:"Always use None as default, then create the mutable inside."},
  {q:"What is the difference between class variables and instance variables?",level:"OOP",difficulty:"Intermediate",experience:"1-5 Years",company:["Amazon","Microsoft","Oracle"],answer:"Class variables are shared by ALL instances (defined in the class body). Instance variables are unique per object (defined with self in __init__).",code:`class Dog:\n    species="Canine"   # class var\n    def __init__(self,name):\n        self.name=name  # instance var\nprint(Dog.species)`,output:`Canine`,tips:"Class vars are shared state; be careful modifying mutable class vars."},
  {q:"What is __init__ method?",level:"OOP",difficulty:"Beginner",experience:"0-4 Years",company:["TCS","Infosys","Amazon"],answer:"__init__ is the constructor — it runs automatically when an object is created, initializing instance attributes. 'self' refers to the instance being created.",code:`class Car:\n    def __init__(self, brand):\n        self.brand = brand\nc=Car("Tesla")\nprint(c.brand)`,output:`Tesla`,tips:"Clarify __init__ initializes, it does NOT create the object (__new__ does)."},
  {q:"What is the difference between __init__ and __new__?",level:"OOP",difficulty:"Expert",experience:"4-10+ Years",company:["Google","Meta","Microsoft"],answer:"__new__ creates and returns the new instance (runs first). __init__ initializes that instance (runs second). You rarely override __new__ except for singletons or immutable types.",tips:"__new__ is a static method that returns the instance; __init__ returns None."},
  {q:"What is the super() function?",level:"OOP",difficulty:"Intermediate",experience:"2-5 Years",company:["Amazon","Adobe","Oracle"],answer:"super() lets a child class call methods of its parent class, commonly in __init__ to reuse the parent's initialization. Essential for cooperative multiple inheritance.",code:`class A:\n    def __init__(self): self.x=1\nclass B(A):\n    def __init__(self):\n        super().__init__()\n        self.y=2\nprint(B().x)`,output:`1`,tips:"super() also respects the MRO in multiple inheritance."},
  {q:"What is MRO (Method Resolution Order)?",level:"OOP",difficulty:"Expert",experience:"4-10+ Years",company:["Google","Meta","Netflix"],answer:"MRO is the order Python searches classes when calling a method in multiple inheritance. Python uses the C3 linearization algorithm. Check it with ClassName.__mro__ or .mro().",code:`class A: pass\nclass B(A): pass\nprint([c.__name__ for c in B.__mro__])`,output:`['B', 'A', 'object']`,tips:"Mention C3 linearization and the diamond problem."},
  {q:"What are magic/dunder methods?",level:"OOP",difficulty:"Advanced",experience:"2-6 Years",company:["Amazon","Adobe","PayPal"],answer:"Dunder (double underscore) methods like __str__, __len__, __add__ let objects work with built-in operations. E.g., __add__ enables the + operator on your objects.",code:`class Vec:\n    def __init__(self,x): self.x=x\n    def __add__(self,o): return Vec(self.x+o.x)\nprint((Vec(2)+Vec(3)).x)`,output:`5`,tips:"__str__ for readable output, __repr__ for debugging — know the difference."},
  {q:"What is the difference between __str__ and __repr__?",level:"OOP",difficulty:"Advanced",experience:"2-6 Years",company:["Google","Adobe"],answer:"__str__ gives a readable, user-friendly string (used by print). __repr__ gives an unambiguous developer string for debugging (used in the REPL/console). If only one is defined, define __repr__.",tips:"Rule: __repr__ for developers, __str__ for users."},
  {q:"What is encapsulation and how is it done in Python?",level:"OOP",difficulty:"Intermediate",experience:"1-5 Years",company:["TCS","Oracle","Cognizant"],answer:"Encapsulation bundles data and methods, restricting direct access. Python uses conventions: _single underscore (protected) and __double underscore (name-mangled/private). Python has no true private.",tips:"Mention Python privacy is by convention, not enforced — 'we're all adults here'."},
  {q:"What is abstraction and how do you implement it?",level:"OOP",difficulty:"Advanced",experience:"2-6 Years",company:["Amazon","Microsoft"],answer:"Abstraction hides complexity, showing only essentials. Implemented via the abc module with abstract base classes and @abstractmethod, forcing subclasses to implement required methods.",code:`from abc import ABC, abstractmethod\nclass Shape(ABC):\n    @abstractmethod\n    def area(self): pass`,output:`# subclasses must implement area()`,tips:"Mention you cannot instantiate an abstract class directly."},
  {q:"What are the SOLID principles?",level:"OOP",difficulty:"Expert",experience:"5-10+ Years",company:["Google","Amazon","Microsoft","Oracle"],answer:"5 design principles: Single Responsibility, Open/Closed, Liskov Substitution, Interface Segregation, Dependency Inversion. They make code maintainable and scalable.",tips:"For senior roles, give a one-line example for each principle."},
  {q:"What is an iterator vs an iterable?",level:"Advanced Python",difficulty:"Advanced",experience:"2-6 Years",company:["Google","Amazon","Netflix"],answer:"An iterable is anything you can loop over (has __iter__). An iterator is the object that produces values one at a time (has __next__). iter() converts iterable to iterator; next() gets the next value.",code:`it=iter([1,2,3])\nprint(next(it), next(it))`,output:`1 2`,tips:"Every iterator is iterable, but not every iterable is an iterator."},
  {q:"What is a generator? How is it different from a normal function?",level:"Advanced Python",difficulty:"Advanced",experience:"2-7 Years",company:["Google","Amazon","Meta","Uber"],answer:"A generator uses 'yield' instead of 'return', producing values lazily one at a time and pausing between them. It saves memory for large datasets since values aren't stored all at once.",code:`def gen():\n    for i in range(3): yield i\nprint(list(gen()))`,output:`[0, 1, 2]`,tips:"Key benefit: memory efficiency for large/infinite sequences."},
  {q:"What is the difference between yield and return?",level:"Advanced Python",difficulty:"Advanced",experience:"2-6 Years",company:["Amazon","Microsoft","Netflix"],answer:"return sends a value and ends the function. yield produces a value but PAUSES the function, resuming from that point on the next call. yield makes a generator.",tips:"Say 'return ends, yield pauses and resumes' — memorable."},
  {q:"What is a context manager and the 'with' statement?",level:"Advanced Python",difficulty:"Advanced",experience:"2-6 Years",company:["Amazon","Google","PayPal"],answer:"A context manager sets up and cleans up resources automatically via __enter__ and __exit__. The 'with' statement ensures cleanup (like closing files) even if an error occurs.",code:`with open("f.txt","w") as f:\n    f.write("hi")\n# file auto-closed`,output:`# file closed automatically`,tips:"Mention contextlib.contextmanager for creating them easily."},
  {q:"What is the difference between multithreading and multiprocessing?",level:"Advanced Python",difficulty:"Expert",experience:"4-10+ Years",company:["Google","Amazon","Uber","Netflix"],answer:"Multithreading runs threads in one process sharing memory — good for I/O-bound tasks but limited by the GIL for CPU tasks. Multiprocessing runs separate processes with separate memory — true parallelism for CPU-bound tasks.",tips:"Threading = I/O-bound, Multiprocessing = CPU-bound. State this clearly."},
  {q:"What is asyncio and when do you use it?",level:"Advanced Python",difficulty:"Expert",experience:"4-10+ Years",company:["Netflix","Uber","Meta"],answer:"asyncio enables asynchronous concurrent code using async/await. One thread handles many I/O operations by switching when one is waiting. Ideal for high-concurrency I/O like web servers and API calls.",code:`import asyncio\nasync def main():\n    await asyncio.sleep(1)\n    print("done")\nasyncio.run(main())`,output:`done`,tips:"Best for thousands of concurrent I/O tasks, not CPU work."},
  {q:"What is garbage collection in Python?",level:"Advanced Python",difficulty:"Expert",experience:"4-10+ Years",company:["Google","Microsoft","Amazon"],answer:"Python frees memory using reference counting (object deleted when refs hit 0) plus a generational cyclic garbage collector that detects reference cycles the counter can't catch.",tips:"Mention the gc module can be tuned or disabled for performance-critical code."},
  {q:"What is monkey patching?",level:"Advanced Python",difficulty:"Expert",experience:"4-10+ Years",company:["Meta","Netflix","Startups"],answer:"Monkey patching means dynamically changing a class or module at runtime (e.g., replacing a method). Used in testing/mocking but risky in production as it can cause hidden bugs.",tips:"Common in unit testing to mock external services."},
  {q:"What is the difference between @staticmethod and @classmethod?",level:"OOP",difficulty:"Advanced",experience:"2-6 Years",company:["Amazon","Adobe","Microsoft"],answer:"@classmethod receives the class (cls) and can access/modify class state, often used as alternate constructors. @staticmethod receives neither self nor cls — it's just a namespaced utility function.",code:`class C:\n    @classmethod\n    def cm(cls): return cls.__name__\n    @staticmethod\n    def sm(): return "static"\nprint(C.cm(), C.sm())`,output:`C static`,tips:"classmethod for factory methods, staticmethod for helpers."},
  {q:"What are Python's namespaces?",level:"Advanced Python",difficulty:"Advanced",experience:"3-7 Years",company:["Google","Microsoft"],answer:"A namespace maps names to objects. Types: built-in, global (module), enclosing, and local (function). They prevent naming conflicts. Python resolves names via the LEGB rule.",tips:"Connect namespaces to the LEGB scope resolution rule."},
  {q:"What is the property decorator?",level:"OOP",difficulty:"Advanced",experience:"2-6 Years",company:["Adobe","Microsoft","PayPal"],answer:"@property turns a method into a read-only attribute, enabling getters/setters while keeping clean attribute-style access. Used for computed values and validation.",code:`class C:\n    def __init__(self,r): self._r=r\n    @property\n    def area(self): return 3.14*self._r**2\nprint(round(C(2).area,2))`,output:`12.56`,tips:"Mention @x.setter for adding validation on assignment."},
  {q:"What is method chaining?",level:"OOP",difficulty:"Intermediate",experience:"2-5 Years",company:["Startups","Adobe"],answer:"Method chaining calls multiple methods in sequence on the same object by having each method return self. Common in builders and pandas.",code:`s="  Hello  "\nprint(s.strip().lower().replace("l","L"))`,output:`heLLo`,tips:"String methods chain naturally since they return new strings."},
  {q:"What is duck typing?",level:"Advanced Python",difficulty:"Advanced",experience:"2-6 Years",company:["Google","Netflix"],answer:"'If it walks like a duck and quacks like a duck, it's a duck.' Python cares about whether an object has the needed method/behavior, not its actual type. Enables flexible polymorphism.",tips:"Contrast with static languages that check types explicitly."},
  {q:"What is the difference between composition and inheritance?",level:"OOP",difficulty:"Advanced",experience:"3-7 Years",company:["Amazon","Microsoft","Google"],answer:"Inheritance is an 'is-a' relationship (Dog is an Animal). Composition is a 'has-a' relationship (Car has an Engine). Prefer composition for flexibility and to avoid deep inheritance chains.",tips:"Say 'favor composition over inheritance' — a well-known principle."}
);


/* ==================== BATCH: DSA & ALGORITHMS ==================== */
window.DP_INTERVIEW.questions.push(
  {q:"What is the difference between a stack and a queue?",level:"Data Structures",difficulty:"Beginner",experience:"0-4 Years",company:["Amazon","Microsoft","TCS"],answer:"A stack is LIFO (Last In First Out) — like a stack of plates. A queue is FIFO (First In First Out) — like a line at a ticket counter.",code:`stack=[]; stack.append(1); stack.append(2)\nprint(stack.pop())`,output:`2`,tips:"Use collections.deque for efficient queues (list.pop(0) is O(n))."},
  {q:"How do you implement a stack in Python?",level:"Data Structures",difficulty:"Beginner",experience:"0-4 Years",company:["Amazon","Zoho","TCS"],answer:"Use a list with append() to push and pop() to remove from the end. Or use collections.deque for thread-safe, efficient operations.",code:`s=[]\ns.append(10); s.append(20)\nprint(s.pop(), s.pop())`,output:`20 10`,tips:"Mention deque is preferred for performance in production."},
  {q:"What is a hash table and how does a dict work internally?",level:"Data Structures",difficulty:"Advanced",experience:"3-7 Years",company:["Google","Amazon","Meta"],answer:"A hash table maps keys to values using a hash function to compute an index. Python dicts use open addressing. Average lookup/insert/delete is O(1). Collisions are handled by probing.",tips:"Mention O(1) average, O(n) worst case for collisions."},
  {q:"What is a binary search tree (BST)?",level:"Data Structures",difficulty:"Advanced",experience:"2-7 Years",company:["Amazon","Google","Microsoft"],answer:"A BST is a tree where each node's left subtree has smaller values and right subtree has larger values. This enables O(log n) search, insert, and delete when balanced.",tips:"Mention that an unbalanced BST degrades to O(n); AVL/Red-Black trees stay balanced."},
  {q:"What is the time complexity of common list operations?",level:"Data Structures",difficulty:"Intermediate",experience:"1-5 Years",company:["Amazon","Google","Adobe"],answer:"Index access O(1), append O(1), insert/delete at position O(n), search 'in' O(n), sort O(n log n). Knowing these guides which data structure to use.",tips:"Memorize this table — it's asked constantly for optimization questions."},
  {q:"How do you find the factorial using recursion and iteration?",level:"Algorithms",difficulty:"Beginner",experience:"0-3 Years",company:["TCS","Wipro","Zoho"],answer:"Recursion: n * factorial(n-1) with base case 1. Iteration: multiply in a loop. Iteration avoids stack overflow for large n.",code:`def fact(n):\n    r=1\n    for i in range(2,n+1): r*=i\n    return r\nprint(fact(5))`,output:`120`,tips:"Mention iteration is safer for large n (no recursion limit)."},
  {q:"Explain binary search and its complexity.",level:"Algorithms",difficulty:"Intermediate",experience:"1-5 Years",company:["Amazon","Google","Microsoft"],answer:"Binary search finds an element in a SORTED array by repeatedly halving the search space. Compare with the middle; go left or right. Time O(log n), space O(1) iterative.",code:`def bs(a,t):\n    lo,hi=0,len(a)-1\n    while lo<=hi:\n        m=(lo+hi)//2\n        if a[m]==t: return m\n        if a[m]<t: lo=m+1\n        else: hi=m-1\n    return -1\nprint(bs([1,3,5,7,9],7))`,output:`3`,tips:"Precondition: array MUST be sorted. State this."},
  {q:"What is the difference between bubble, merge, and quick sort?",level:"Algorithms",difficulty:"Advanced",experience:"2-6 Years",company:["Amazon","Google","Adobe"],answer:"Bubble sort O(n^2) - simple but slow. Merge sort O(n log n) - stable, uses O(n) extra space. Quick sort O(n log n) average - in-place but O(n^2) worst case. Python's sort uses Timsort.",tips:"Mention Python's built-in sort is Timsort (hybrid merge+insertion)."},
  {q:"What is dynamic programming?",level:"Algorithms",difficulty:"Expert",experience:"3-10+ Years",company:["Google","Amazon","Meta","Uber"],answer:"DP solves problems by breaking them into overlapping subproblems and storing results (memoization) to avoid recomputation. Used in Fibonacci, knapsack, longest common subsequence.",code:`from functools import lru_cache\n@lru_cache\ndef fib(n): return n if n<2 else fib(n-1)+fib(n-2)\nprint(fib(30))`,output:`832040`,tips:"Mention @lru_cache as an easy memoization tool."},
  {q:"What is the difference between BFS and DFS?",level:"Algorithms",difficulty:"Advanced",experience:"2-7 Years",company:["Google","Amazon","Meta"],answer:"BFS (Breadth-First) explores level by level using a queue — finds shortest path in unweighted graphs. DFS (Depth-First) goes deep first using a stack/recursion — good for path existence and cycle detection.",tips:"BFS=queue+shortest path, DFS=stack/recursion+deep exploration."},
  {q:"How do you detect a cycle in a linked list?",level:"Data Structures",difficulty:"Advanced",experience:"2-7 Years",company:["Amazon","Google","Microsoft","Uber"],answer:"Use Floyd's Tortoise and Hare: two pointers, slow moves 1 step, fast moves 2 steps. If they meet, there's a cycle. Time O(n), space O(1).",tips:"Name-drop 'Floyd's cycle detection' — shows algorithm knowledge."},
  {q:"What is Big O notation?",level:"Algorithms",difficulty:"Intermediate",experience:"1-5 Years",company:["Amazon","Google","TCS","Adobe"],answer:"Big O describes how an algorithm's time/space grows as input grows, focusing on the worst case. Common: O(1) constant, O(log n) logarithmic, O(n) linear, O(n log n), O(n^2) quadratic.",tips:"Always analyze BOTH time and space complexity when solving coding problems."},
  {q:"How do you remove duplicates from a list preserving order?",level:"Coding Problems",difficulty:"Beginner",experience:"0-4 Years",company:["Amazon","Zoho","TCS"],answer:"Use dict.fromkeys() which preserves insertion order (Python 3.7+), or a loop with a seen set. Plain set() loses order.",code:`nums=[1,3,2,3,1]\nprint(list(dict.fromkeys(nums)))`,output:`[1, 3, 2]`,tips:"set() is fastest but loses order; dict.fromkeys keeps it."},
  {q:"How do you find the maximum subarray sum (Kadane's algorithm)?",level:"Coding Problems",difficulty:"Advanced",experience:"2-7 Years",company:["Amazon","Google","Microsoft"],answer:"Kadane's algorithm: track current_sum (reset to 0 or current if negative) and max_sum. One pass, O(n) time, O(1) space.",code:`def max_sub(a):\n    cur=best=a[0]\n    for n in a[1:]:\n        cur=max(n,cur+n)\n        best=max(best,cur)\n    return best\nprint(max_sub([-2,1,-3,4,-1,2,1,-5,4]))`,output:`6`,tips:"This is a classic — memorize the two-variable pattern."},
  {q:"How do you check if two strings are anagrams?",level:"Coding Problems",difficulty:"Beginner",experience:"0-4 Years",company:["Amazon","Adobe","Zoho"],answer:"Sort both and compare, OR compare character counts with Counter. Counter is O(n) vs sorting's O(n log n).",code:`from collections import Counter\ndef anagram(a,b): return Counter(a)==Counter(b)\nprint(anagram("listen","silent"))`,output:`True`,tips:"Counter approach is O(n) — mention it beats sorting."},
  {q:"How do you find the first non-repeating character?",level:"Coding Problems",difficulty:"Intermediate",experience:"1-5 Years",company:["Amazon","Microsoft","Zoho"],answer:"Count character frequencies with Counter, then scan the string for the first char with count 1. O(n) time.",code:`from collections import Counter\ndef first_uniq(s):\n    c=Counter(s)\n    for ch in s:\n        if c[ch]==1: return ch\n    return None\nprint(first_uniq("aabbc"))`,output:`c`,tips:"Two passes but still O(n) — explain why two passes are fine."},
  {q:"How do you find the GCD of two numbers?",level:"Coding Problems",difficulty:"Beginner",experience:"0-4 Years",company:["TCS","Wipro","Zoho"],answer:"Use the Euclidean algorithm: gcd(a,b) = gcd(b, a%b) until b is 0. Or use math.gcd().",code:`import math\nprint(math.gcd(48, 36))`,output:`12`,tips:"Know both the manual Euclidean method AND math.gcd()."},
  {q:"How do you check if a number is prime?",level:"Coding Problems",difficulty:"Beginner",experience:"0-4 Years",company:["TCS","Zoho","Wipro"],answer:"Check divisibility from 2 up to sqrt(n). If any divides evenly, it's not prime. Checking only to sqrt(n) is the key optimization.",code:`def is_prime(n):\n    if n<2: return False\n    for i in range(2,int(n**0.5)+1):\n        if n%i==0: return False\n    return True\nprint(is_prime(29))`,output:`True`,tips:"The sqrt(n) optimization is what interviewers look for."},
  {q:"How do you find duplicates in an array?",level:"Coding Problems",difficulty:"Intermediate",experience:"1-5 Years",company:["Amazon","Adobe","Cognizant"],answer:"Use a Counter and filter items with count > 1, or track a seen set and report items seen twice. O(n) time.",code:`from collections import Counter\na=[1,2,2,3,3,3]\nprint([k for k,v in Counter(a).items() if v>1])`,output:`[2, 3]`,tips:"Counter is the cleanest one-liner approach."},
  {q:"How do you merge two sorted lists?",level:"Coding Problems",difficulty:"Intermediate",experience:"1-6 Years",company:["Amazon","Google","Microsoft"],answer:"Use two pointers, comparing the front of each list and appending the smaller one. O(n+m) time. This is the merge step of merge sort.",tips:"Mention this is the core of merge sort's merge operation."},
  {q:"How do you find the second largest element in a list?",level:"Coding Problems",difficulty:"Beginner",experience:"0-4 Years",company:["TCS","Zoho","Amazon"],answer:"Track two variables (largest, second) in one pass, OR use sorted(set(list))[-2]. The one-pass approach is O(n) without sorting.",code:`a=[10,5,8,20,15]\nprint(sorted(set(a))[-2])`,output:`15`,tips:"One-pass with two variables is O(n) vs sorting O(n log n)."},
  {q:"How do you reverse words in a sentence?",level:"Coding Problems",difficulty:"Beginner",experience:"0-4 Years",company:["Amazon","Zoho","TCS"],answer:"Split into words, reverse the list, and join back with spaces.",code:`s="Python is fun"\nprint(" ".join(s.split()[::-1]))`,output:`fun is Python`,tips:"split() without args also handles multiple spaces nicely."},
  {q:"What is the two-pointer technique?",level:"Algorithms",difficulty:"Advanced",experience:"2-7 Years",company:["Amazon","Google","Meta"],answer:"Two pointers move through data from different positions (both ends, or slow/fast) to solve problems in O(n) instead of O(n^2). Used in sorted-array problems, palindromes, and removing duplicates.",tips:"Great for 'pair sum in sorted array' and palindrome checks."},
  {q:"What is the sliding window technique?",level:"Algorithms",difficulty:"Advanced",experience:"3-8 Years",company:["Google","Amazon","Meta","Uber"],answer:"Sliding window maintains a subset (window) of elements and slides it to avoid recomputation. Used for 'max sum subarray of size k' or 'longest substring without repeats' in O(n).",tips:"Recognize keywords like 'subarray/substring of size k' — signals sliding window."},
  {q:"How do you flatten a nested list?",level:"Coding Problems",difficulty:"Intermediate",experience:"1-5 Years",company:["Amazon","Adobe","Startups"],answer:"Use a list comprehension for one level of nesting, or recursion for arbitrary depth.",code:`nested=[[1,2],[3,4],[5]]\nflat=[x for sub in nested for x in sub]\nprint(flat)`,output:`[1, 2, 3, 4, 5]`,tips:"For deep nesting, use recursion or itertools.chain."},
  {q:"How do you count word frequency in a paragraph?",level:"Coding Problems",difficulty:"Beginner",experience:"0-5 Years",company:["Amazon","Google","Zoho"],answer:"Lowercase and split the text, then use collections.Counter to count words instantly.",code:`from collections import Counter\ntext="the cat the dog the bird"\nprint(Counter(text.split()).most_common(1))`,output:`[('the', 3)]`,tips:"most_common(n) returns the top n — very handy."},
  {q:"How do you implement a queue using two stacks?",level:"Data Structures",difficulty:"Advanced",experience:"3-8 Years",company:["Amazon","Microsoft","Google"],answer:"Use an 'in' stack for enqueue and an 'out' stack for dequeue. When 'out' is empty, pour all of 'in' into 'out' (reversing order to achieve FIFO). Amortized O(1).",tips:"Classic design question — explain the amortized O(1) analysis."},
  {q:"What is memoization vs tabulation in DP?",level:"Algorithms",difficulty:"Expert",experience:"4-10+ Years",company:["Google","Amazon","Meta"],answer:"Memoization is top-down (recursion + cache). Tabulation is bottom-up (build a table iteratively). Memoization is intuitive; tabulation avoids recursion overhead and stack limits.",tips:"Both are O(n) time; tabulation often saves stack space."}
);


/* ==================== BATCH: LIBRARIES, DB, API, TESTING, SCENARIO, HR ==================== */
window.DP_INTERVIEW.questions.push(
  {q:"What is the difference between a list and a NumPy array?",level:"Libraries",difficulty:"Intermediate",experience:"1-5 Years",company:["Amazon","Adobe","Startups"],answer:"NumPy arrays are fixed-type, stored contiguously in memory, and support vectorized operations (fast, no loops). Lists are flexible (mixed types) but slower for numeric work.",code:`import numpy as np\na=np.array([1,2,3])\nprint(a*2)`,output:`[2 4 6]`,tips:"Mention vectorization makes NumPy 10-100x faster for math."},
  {q:"What is a Pandas DataFrame?",level:"Libraries",difficulty:"Intermediate",experience:"1-5 Years",company:["Amazon","Adobe","Walmart Global Tech"],answer:"A DataFrame is a 2D labeled table (rows and columns) in Pandas, like an Excel sheet or SQL table. It's the core structure for data analysis in Python.",code:`import pandas as pd\ndf=pd.DataFrame({"a":[1,2],"b":[3,4]})\nprint(df["a"].sum())`,output:`3`,tips:"Mention common ops: groupby, merge, filtering with boolean masks."},
  {q:"What is the difference between Flask and Django?",level:"Libraries",difficulty:"Intermediate",experience:"2-6 Years",company:["Startups","PayPal","Freshworks"],answer:"Flask is a lightweight micro-framework — flexible, minimal, you choose components. Django is a full-featured 'batteries-included' framework with ORM, admin, auth built in. Flask for small/custom apps, Django for large ones.",tips:"Mention FastAPI as the modern high-performance alternative."},
  {q:"What is an ORM?",level:"Database",difficulty:"Intermediate",experience:"2-6 Years",company:["PayPal","Startups","Oracle"],answer:"Object-Relational Mapping lets you interact with a database using Python objects instead of raw SQL. E.g., SQLAlchemy or Django ORM. It prevents SQL injection and speeds development.",tips:"Mention it abstracts SQL but you should still understand raw SQL."},
  {q:"What is the difference between SQL and NoSQL databases?",level:"Database",difficulty:"Intermediate",experience:"2-6 Years",company:["Amazon","Google","Uber"],answer:"SQL (MySQL, PostgreSQL) is relational with fixed schemas and ACID guarantees — great for structured data. NoSQL (MongoDB, Redis) is flexible/schema-less and scales horizontally — great for unstructured or huge data.",tips:"SQL=structured+ACID, NoSQL=flexible+scalable. Match to use case."},
  {q:"What is a parameterized query and why use it?",level:"Database",difficulty:"Advanced",experience:"2-7 Years",company:["Amazon","PayPal","Oracle"],answer:"A parameterized query uses placeholders (?) for values instead of string concatenation. It prevents SQL injection attacks and improves performance via query caching.",code:`cur.execute("SELECT * FROM users WHERE id=?", (user_id,))`,output:`# safe from SQL injection`,tips:"NEVER build SQL with f-strings/concatenation — always parameterize."},
  {q:"What is a REST API?",level:"APIs",difficulty:"Intermediate",experience:"1-6 Years",company:["Amazon","PayPal","Uber","Startups"],answer:"REST is an architectural style for web APIs using HTTP methods: GET (read), POST (create), PUT (update), DELETE (remove). It's stateless and uses JSON for data exchange.",tips:"Know the HTTP verbs and status codes (200, 201, 404, 500)."},
  {q:"What is the difference between PUT and PATCH?",level:"APIs",difficulty:"Advanced",experience:"2-6 Years",company:["PayPal","Uber","Amazon"],answer:"PUT replaces the ENTIRE resource (full update). PATCH updates only the specified FIELDS (partial update). PUT is idempotent; PATCH may or may not be.",tips:"PUT=full replace, PATCH=partial update. Common API question."},
  {q:"What is JWT and how does authentication work?",level:"APIs",difficulty:"Advanced",experience:"2-7 Years",company:["PayPal","Uber","Startups"],answer:"JWT (JSON Web Token) is a signed token containing user data. On login the server issues a token; the client sends it in the Authorization header on each request. The server verifies the signature — stateless auth.",tips:"Mention it's stateless (no server session storage needed)."},
  {q:"What is the difference between authentication and authorization?",level:"APIs",difficulty:"Intermediate",experience:"1-6 Years",company:["Amazon","PayPal","Microsoft"],answer:"Authentication verifies WHO you are (login). Authorization verifies WHAT you're allowed to do (permissions). Authentication comes first, then authorization.",tips:"AuthN = identity, AuthZ = permissions. Simple mnemonic."},
  {q:"What is the difference between unittest and pytest?",level:"Testing",difficulty:"Intermediate",experience:"1-6 Years",company:["Google","Amazon","Microsoft"],answer:"unittest is Python's built-in xUnit-style framework (class-based, verbose). pytest is a third-party framework with simpler syntax (plain assert), fixtures, and better output. Most teams prefer pytest.",code:`def test_add():\n    assert 2+3 == 5`,output:`1 passed`,tips:"pytest's plain 'assert' and fixtures make it the modern choice."},
  {q:"What is mocking in testing?",level:"Testing",difficulty:"Advanced",experience:"2-7 Years",company:["Amazon","Google","PayPal"],answer:"Mocking replaces real objects (like APIs, databases) with fake ones during tests, so you test your code in isolation without external dependencies. Use unittest.mock or pytest-mock.",tips:"Mock external calls (APIs, DB) to make tests fast and reliable."},
  {q:"What are pytest fixtures?",level:"Testing",difficulty:"Advanced",experience:"2-6 Years",company:["Google","Netflix","Startups"],answer:"Fixtures provide reusable setup/teardown for tests (like a database connection or test data). Marked with @pytest.fixture and injected into test functions as arguments.",tips:"Fixtures reduce duplication and manage test resources cleanly."},
  {q:"What is test-driven development (TDD)?",level:"Testing",difficulty:"Advanced",experience:"3-8 Years",company:["Google","Microsoft","ThoughtWorks"],answer:"TDD is: write a failing test first (Red), write minimal code to pass (Green), then improve the code (Refactor). It ensures testable design and good coverage.",tips:"Red-Green-Refactor is the TDD cycle — name it."},
  {q:"SCENARIO: Your Python script is slow. How do you optimize it?",level:"Scenario-Based",difficulty:"Expert",experience:"3-10+ Years",company:["Google","Amazon","Netflix"],answer:"1. Profile first (cProfile) to find the bottleneck. 2. Use better algorithms/data structures. 3. Use built-ins and vectorization (NumPy). 4. Cache with lru_cache. 5. Use multiprocessing for CPU-bound work. Never optimize without measuring.",tips:"Always say 'profile first' — optimizing blindly is a red flag."},
  {q:"SCENARIO: How do you handle a memory leak in a long-running Python app?",level:"Scenario-Based",difficulty:"Expert",experience:"4-10+ Years",company:["Google","Uber","Netflix"],answer:"Use tracemalloc or objgraph to find growing objects. Common causes: global caches without limits, circular references, unclosed resources. Fix with weak references, bounded caches, and context managers.",tips:"Name real tools: tracemalloc, objgraph, memory_profiler."},
  {q:"SCENARIO: How do you debug a production issue you can't reproduce locally?",level:"Scenario-Based",difficulty:"Expert",experience:"3-10+ Years",company:["Amazon","Netflix","Uber"],answer:"Check logs and monitoring/metrics first. Add structured logging around the suspect area. Reproduce with production-like data. Use feature flags to isolate. Check recent deploys/config changes.",tips:"Emphasize logging and observability — senior engineers rely on them."},
  {q:"SCENARIO: A colleague's code works but is unreadable. What do you do in code review?",level:"Scenario-Based",difficulty:"Advanced",experience:"2-8 Years",company:["Google","Microsoft","ThoughtWorks"],answer:"Give constructive, specific feedback: suggest better names, breaking large functions, adding docstrings. Praise what's good. Focus on the code, not the person. Reference style guides (PEP 8).",tips:"Show empathy + professionalism — soft skills matter for senior roles."},
  {q:"SCENARIO: How would you design a URL shortener like bit.ly?",level:"Scenario-Based",difficulty:"Expert",experience:"4-10+ Years",company:["Amazon","Google","Uber"],answer:"Generate a short unique code (base62 of an auto-increment ID or hash), store code->URL mapping in a DB, redirect on lookup. Add caching (Redis), analytics, and handle collisions. Discuss scale and read-heavy optimization.",tips:"System design: discuss data model, encoding, caching, and scaling."},
  {q:"What is the difference between deep and shallow debugging with pdb?",level:"Scenario-Based",difficulty:"Advanced",experience:"2-7 Years",company:["Amazon","Microsoft"],answer:"pdb is Python's debugger. Set breakpoints with breakpoint(). Commands: n (next), s (step into), c (continue), p (print variable), l (list code). Step through to inspect state.",tips:"Know the key pdb commands: n, s, c, p, q."},
  /* More HR */
  {q:"Why do you want to work at our company?",level:"HR & Behavioural",difficulty:"Beginner",experience:"0-10+ Years",company:["All Companies"],answer:"Research the company first. Connect their mission/products/tech to your skills and goals. Show genuine interest, not generic praise. Mention something specific about them.",tips:"Name a specific product, value, or project of theirs — proves you researched."},
  {q:"What are your strengths?",level:"HR & Behavioural",difficulty:"Beginner",experience:"0-10+ Years",company:["All Companies"],answer:"Pick 2-3 strengths relevant to the role, and back EACH with a concrete example/achievement. E.g., 'problem-solving — I optimized a script that cut runtime 60%.'",tips:"Never list strengths without proof — always give a real example."},
  {q:"Where do you see yourself in 5 years?",level:"HR & Behavioural",difficulty:"Beginner",experience:"0-10+ Years",company:["All Companies"],answer:"Show ambition + commitment. Talk about growing technically (senior engineer, mentoring, mastering a domain) aligned with a path the company offers. Avoid unrealistic jumps.",tips:"Align your growth with the company's opportunities — shows you'll stay."},
  {q:"Why should we hire you?",level:"HR & Behavioural",difficulty:"Intermediate",experience:"0-10+ Years",company:["All Companies"],answer:"Summarize your top relevant skills, a proven achievement, and cultural fit. Be confident, specific, and tie it directly to what the role needs.",tips:"This is your elevator pitch — practice a crisp 30-second version."},
  {q:"Tell me about a challenge you faced and how you solved it.",level:"HR & Behavioural",difficulty:"Intermediate",experience:"1-10+ Years",company:["Amazon","Google","All Companies"],answer:"Use the STAR method: Situation, Task, Action, Result. Pick a real technical challenge, explain your specific actions, and quantify the positive result.",tips:"STAR method + a measurable result (e.g., 'reduced errors by 40%')."},
  {q:"How do you handle tight deadlines and pressure?",level:"HR & Behavioural",difficulty:"Intermediate",experience:"0-10+ Years",company:["All Companies"],answer:"Prioritize tasks, communicate early about risks, break work into chunks, and focus on the highest-impact items first. Give a real example where you delivered under pressure.",tips:"Show you stay calm, prioritize, and communicate — not that you panic."},
  {q:"What is your expected salary?",level:"HR & Behavioural",difficulty:"Intermediate",experience:"0-10+ Years",company:["All Companies"],answer:"Research market rates for the role/location/experience first. Give a range, stay flexible, and emphasize you're open to discussion based on the total package.",tips:"Give a researched range, not a single number; mention total compensation."},
  {q:"Do you have any questions for us?",level:"HR & Behavioural",difficulty:"Beginner",experience:"0-10+ Years",company:["All Companies"],answer:"Always say YES. Ask about the team, tech stack, growth opportunities, or what success looks like in the role. It shows genuine interest.",tips:"Never say 'no questions' — prepare 2-3 thoughtful ones in advance."},
  {q:"How do you keep your Python skills up to date?",level:"HR & Behavioural",difficulty:"Intermediate",experience:"1-10+ Years",company:["Google","Netflix","Startups"],answer:"Mention concrete habits: reading docs/PEPs, following Python blogs, building side projects, contributing to open source, and taking courses. Show continuous learning.",tips:"Name specific resources — real learning habits stand out."},
  {q:"Describe a time you disagreed with a teammate.",level:"HR & Behavioural",difficulty:"Advanced",experience:"2-10+ Years",company:["Amazon","Google","Meta"],answer:"Use STAR. Show you listened, presented data/reasoning, stayed respectful, and reached a good outcome together. Emphasize collaboration over winning.",tips:"Focus on resolution and respect, not on 'being right'."}
);


/* ==================== BATCH: COLLECTIONS, CONTROL FLOW, FILES ==================== */
window.DP_INTERVIEW.questions.push(
  {q:"What is a list comprehension?",level:"Collections",difficulty:"Beginner",experience:"0-4 Years",company:["Amazon","Zoho","Infosys"],answer:"A concise one-line way to create a list: [expression for item in iterable if condition]. Faster and more readable than a for loop with append.",code:`squares=[x*x for x in range(5)]\nprint(squares)`,output:`[0, 1, 4, 9, 16]`,tips:"Don't over-nest — if it's hard to read, use a regular loop."},
  {q:"What is a dictionary comprehension?",level:"Collections",difficulty:"Intermediate",experience:"1-4 Years",company:["Amazon","Adobe","Zoho"],answer:"Creates a dict in one line: {key: value for item in iterable}. Great for transforming or filtering data.",code:`sq={x:x*x for x in range(4)}\nprint(sq)`,output:`{0: 0, 1: 1, 2: 4, 3: 9}`,tips:"Useful for building lookup tables quickly."},
  {q:"What is a set and when do you use it?",level:"Collections",difficulty:"Beginner",experience:"0-4 Years",company:["Amazon","Zoho","TCS"],answer:"A set is an unordered collection of UNIQUE elements. Use it to remove duplicates and for fast O(1) membership tests and set operations (union, intersection).",code:`print(set([1,1,2,3,3]))`,output:`{1, 2, 3}`,tips:"O(1) lookup makes sets ideal for 'have I seen this?' checks."},
  {q:"What are set operations (union, intersection, difference)?",level:"Collections",difficulty:"Intermediate",experience:"1-4 Years",company:["Amazon","Adobe"],answer:"union (|) combines all elements, intersection (&) keeps common ones, difference (-) keeps items in the first not the second, symmetric_difference (^) keeps items in exactly one.",code:`a={1,2,3}; b={2,3,4}\nprint(a&b, a|b, a-b)`,output:`{2, 3} {1, 2, 3, 4} {1}`,tips:"Great for comparing two datasets (e.g., common users)."},
  {q:"What is a frozenset?",level:"Collections",difficulty:"Intermediate",experience:"2-5 Years",company:["Microsoft","Adobe"],answer:"A frozenset is an immutable set. Because it's hashable, it can be used as a dictionary key or an element of another set, unlike a regular set.",tips:"Use when you need an immutable/hashable set (e.g., dict keys)."},
  {q:"How do you merge two dictionaries?",level:"Collections",difficulty:"Beginner",experience:"1-4 Years",company:["Amazon","Zoho","Startups"],answer:"Python 3.9+: use the | operator. Earlier: {**a, **b} or a.update(b). Later keys override earlier ones.",code:`a={"x":1}; b={"y":2}\nprint(a|b)`,output:`{'x': 1, 'y': 2}`,tips:"Mention {**a, **b} for older Python versions."},
  {q:"What is the difference between get() and [] for dict access?",level:"Collections",difficulty:"Beginner",experience:"0-4 Years",company:["TCS","Zoho","Amazon"],answer:"dict[key] raises KeyError if the key is missing. dict.get(key, default) returns a default (None or specified) instead — safer for optional keys.",code:`d={"a":1}\nprint(d.get("z","none"))`,output:`none`,tips:"Use get() to avoid try/except for missing keys."},
  {q:"What is defaultdict?",level:"Collections",difficulty:"Intermediate",experience:"1-5 Years",company:["Amazon","Google","Adobe"],answer:"defaultdict (from collections) auto-creates a default value for missing keys, avoiding KeyError. E.g., defaultdict(list) gives an empty list for new keys — great for grouping.",code:`from collections import defaultdict\nd=defaultdict(int)\nfor c in "aab": d[c]+=1\nprint(dict(d))`,output:`{'a': 2, 'b': 1}`,tips:"Perfect for counting and grouping without key checks."},
  {q:"What is an OrderedDict? Is it still needed?",level:"Collections",difficulty:"Intermediate",experience:"2-5 Years",company:["Microsoft","Adobe"],answer:"OrderedDict remembers insertion order. Since Python 3.7 regular dicts also preserve order, so it's rarely needed now — except for its move_to_end() method and order-sensitive equality.",tips:"Note regular dicts are ordered since 3.7 — shows you're current."},
  {q:"What does the enumerate() function do?",level:"Control Flow",difficulty:"Beginner",experience:"0-4 Years",company:["Amazon","Zoho","TCS"],answer:"enumerate() adds an index counter while looping, returning (index, value) pairs. Cleaner than manually tracking an index variable.",code:`for i,v in enumerate(["a","b"]):\n    print(i,v)`,output:`0 a\n1 b`,tips:"Use start=1 for 1-based indexing: enumerate(x, 1)."},
  {q:"What is the difference between break, continue, and pass?",level:"Control Flow",difficulty:"Beginner",experience:"0-3 Years",company:["TCS","Infosys","Zoho"],answer:"break exits the loop entirely. continue skips to the next iteration. pass does nothing (placeholder). They control loop flow differently.",tips:"break=exit, continue=skip, pass=nothing. Crisp distinction."},
  {q:"What is the for-else construct?",level:"Control Flow",difficulty:"Advanced",experience:"2-6 Years",company:["Google","Microsoft"],answer:"The else block after a for loop runs ONLY if the loop completed without hitting break. Useful for search loops: else means 'not found'.",code:`for x in [1,2,3]:\n    if x==5: break\nelse:\n    print("not found")`,output:`not found`,tips:"A lesser-known feature — impresses interviewers when used well."},
  {q:"What is match-case in Python?",level:"Control Flow",difficulty:"Intermediate",experience:"2-5 Years",company:["Microsoft","Google"],answer:"match-case (Python 3.10+) is structural pattern matching, similar to switch but more powerful — it can match values, types, and destructure data.",code:`x=2\nmatch x:\n    case 1: print("one")\n    case 2: print("two")\n    case _: print("other")`,output:`two`,tips:"Mention it can destructure tuples/objects, not just match values."},
  {q:"How do you read a file line by line efficiently?",level:"File Handling",difficulty:"Beginner",experience:"0-4 Years",company:["Amazon","TCS","Startups"],answer:"Iterate directly over the file object inside a 'with' block. This is memory-efficient because it reads one line at a time instead of loading the whole file.",code:`with open("f.txt") as f:\n    for line in f:\n        print(line.strip())`,output:`# prints each line`,tips:"Avoid f.readlines() for large files — it loads everything into memory."},
  {q:"What are file modes in Python?",level:"File Handling",difficulty:"Beginner",experience:"0-4 Years",company:["TCS","Infosys","Zoho"],answer:"'r' read, 'w' write (overwrites), 'a' append, 'r+' read/write, 'b' binary (e.g., 'rb'). Add '+' for read-write. Default is 'r' text mode.",tips:"Warn that 'w' overwrites existing content — use 'a' to append."},
  {q:"Why use 'with' when opening files?",level:"File Handling",difficulty:"Beginner",experience:"0-4 Years",company:["Amazon","Google","TCS"],answer:"'with' is a context manager that automatically closes the file even if an error occurs. It prevents resource leaks and is the recommended, clean way to handle files.",tips:"Always prefer 'with open(...)' over manual open/close."},
  {q:"What is the difference between JSON and pickle?",level:"File Handling",difficulty:"Advanced",experience:"2-6 Years",company:["Amazon","Netflix","PayPal"],answer:"JSON is text-based, human-readable, language-independent (only basic types). Pickle is Python-specific binary serialization (any Python object) but insecure with untrusted data.",tips:"Never unpickle untrusted data — it can execute arbitrary code."},
  {q:"How do you read and write a CSV file?",level:"File Handling",difficulty:"Intermediate",experience:"1-5 Years",company:["Amazon","Walmart Global Tech","Adobe"],answer:"Use the csv module: csv.reader/writer for lists, or csv.DictReader/DictWriter for dicts. For data analysis, pandas.read_csv is more powerful.",code:`import csv\nwith open("d.csv","w",newline="") as f:\n    csv.writer(f).writerow(["a","b"])`,output:`# writes a CSV row`,tips:"Use newline='' to avoid blank rows on Windows."},
  {q:"What is the difference between read(), readline(), and readlines()?",level:"File Handling",difficulty:"Intermediate",experience:"1-4 Years",company:["TCS","Amazon"],answer:"read() returns the whole file as one string. readline() reads one line. readlines() returns a list of all lines. For big files, iterate the file object instead.",tips:"read() and readlines() load everything — avoid for large files."},
  /* More coding */
  {q:"How do you find the intersection of two lists?",level:"Coding Problems",difficulty:"Beginner",experience:"0-4 Years",company:["Amazon","Zoho","Adobe"],answer:"Convert to sets and use & , or use a comprehension. Sets give O(n) instead of O(n^2).",code:`a=[1,2,3]; b=[2,3,4]\nprint(list(set(a)&set(b)))`,output:`[2, 3]`,tips:"Set intersection is O(n) vs nested-loop O(n^2)."},
  {q:"How do you check if a string is a palindrome (ignoring case/spaces)?",level:"Coding Problems",difficulty:"Intermediate",experience:"1-5 Years",company:["Amazon","Adobe","Zoho"],answer:"Normalize: lowercase and remove non-alphanumeric characters, then compare with its reverse.",code:`s="A man a plan a canal Panama"\nc="".join(ch.lower() for ch in s if ch.isalnum())\nprint(c==c[::-1])`,output:`True`,tips:"Clarify with interviewer whether to ignore spaces/case first."},
  {q:"How do you find the most frequent element in a list?",level:"Coding Problems",difficulty:"Beginner",experience:"0-5 Years",company:["Amazon","Google","Zoho"],answer:"Use Counter(list).most_common(1) to get the top element and its count in O(n).",code:`from collections import Counter\nprint(Counter([1,2,2,3,2]).most_common(1))`,output:`[(2, 3)]`,tips:"most_common(1) returns a list of one (value, count) tuple."},
  {q:"How do you sort a list of dictionaries by a key?",level:"Coding Problems",difficulty:"Intermediate",experience:"1-5 Years",company:["Amazon","Adobe","Walmart Global Tech"],answer:"Use sorted() with a lambda key: sorted(data, key=lambda d: d['age']). Add reverse=True for descending.",code:`p=[{"n":"A","age":30},{"n":"B","age":25}]\nprint(sorted(p,key=lambda x:x["age"])[0]["n"])`,output:`B`,tips:"key=lambda is asked constantly — know it cold."},
  {q:"How do you count vowels and consonants in a string?",level:"Coding Problems",difficulty:"Beginner",experience:"0-4 Years",company:["TCS","Wipro","Zoho"],answer:"Loop through characters, check membership in 'aeiou' for vowels; alphabetic non-vowels are consonants.",code:`s="hello"\nv=sum(1 for c in s.lower() if c in "aeiou")\nprint("vowels:",v)`,output:`vowels: 2`,tips:"Use .lower() and .isalpha() to handle case and symbols."},
  {q:"How do you generate the Fibonacci series?",level:"Coding Problems",difficulty:"Beginner",experience:"0-4 Years",company:["TCS","Infosys","Amazon"],answer:"Iteratively track two variables a,b and update a,b=b,a+b. O(n) time, O(1) space — better than naive recursion.",code:`a,b=0,1\nfor _ in range(7):\n    print(a,end=" ")\n    a,b=b,a+b`,output:`0 1 1 2 3 5 8 `,tips:"Iterative beats naive recursion (which is O(2^n))."},
  {q:"How do you check if two lists have common elements?",level:"Coding Problems",difficulty:"Beginner",experience:"0-4 Years",company:["Zoho","TCS","Amazon"],answer:"Convert to sets and check if their intersection is non-empty, or use set.isdisjoint() (returns True if NO common elements).",code:`a=[1,2]; b=[3,2]\nprint(bool(set(a)&set(b)))`,output:`True`,tips:"not set(a).isdisjoint(b) is the cleanest check."},
  {q:"How do you remove all whitespace from a string?",level:"Coding Problems",difficulty:"Beginner",experience:"0-3 Years",company:["TCS","Zoho"],answer:"Use ''.join(s.split()) to remove all whitespace, or s.replace(' ','') for spaces only, or re.sub for all whitespace types.",code:`s="a b  c"\nprint("".join(s.split()))`,output:`abc`,tips:"split() handles tabs and multiple spaces automatically."},
  {q:"How do you find the length of the longest word in a sentence?",level:"Coding Problems",difficulty:"Beginner",experience:"0-4 Years",company:["Zoho","TCS","Amazon"],answer:"Split into words and use max() with key=len to find the longest.",code:`s="Python is awesome"\nprint(max(s.split(),key=len))`,output:`awesome`,tips:"max(key=len) is elegant — avoids manual loops."},
  {q:"How do you convert a list of tuples into a dictionary?",level:"Coding Problems",difficulty:"Beginner",experience:"0-4 Years",company:["Amazon","Zoho"],answer:"Pass the list of (key, value) tuples directly to dict().",code:`pairs=[("a",1),("b",2)]\nprint(dict(pairs))`,output:`{'a': 1, 'b': 2}`,tips:"dict(zip(keys, values)) is a related common pattern."},
  {q:"How do you capitalize the first letter of each word?",level:"Coding Problems",difficulty:"Beginner",experience:"0-3 Years",company:["TCS","Zoho"],answer:"Use str.title() for simple cases, or ' '.join(w.capitalize() for w in s.split()) for more control.",code:`print("hello world".title())`,output:`Hello World`,tips:"title() has quirks with apostrophes — mention capitalize per word as alternative."},
  {q:"How do you check if a year is a leap year?",level:"Coding Problems",difficulty:"Beginner",experience:"0-3 Years",company:["TCS","Wipro","Infosys"],answer:"A leap year is divisible by 4, but NOT by 100 unless also divisible by 400.",code:`y=2000\nprint(y%4==0 and (y%100!=0 or y%400==0))`,output:`True`,tips:"The 100/400 rule is the tricky part interviewers check."},
  {q:"How do you find common characters in two strings?",level:"Coding Problems",difficulty:"Intermediate",experience:"1-5 Years",company:["Amazon","Adobe","Zoho"],answer:"Convert both to sets and intersect, or use Counter for frequency-aware common characters.",code:`a="hello"; b="world"\nprint(set(a)&set(b))`,output:`{'l', 'o'}`,tips:"Set for unique common chars; Counter if counts matter."},
  {q:"How do you swap case of all letters in a string?",level:"Coding Problems",difficulty:"Beginner",experience:"0-3 Years",company:["Zoho","TCS"],answer:"Use the built-in str.swapcase() method which flips upper to lower and vice versa.",code:`print("Hello World".swapcase())`,output:`hELLO wORLD`,tips:"Know the built-in exists — no need to loop manually."},
  {q:"How do you sum digits of a number until a single digit (digital root)?",level:"Coding Problems",difficulty:"Intermediate",experience:"1-5 Years",company:["Amazon","Zoho","Adobe"],answer:"Repeatedly sum the digits until one digit remains. Math trick: digital root = 1 + (n-1) % 9 for n > 0.",code:`n=9875\nwhile n>9:\n    n=sum(int(d) for d in str(n))\nprint(n)`,output:`2`,tips:"Mention the O(1) formula (1+(n-1)%9) to impress."}
);


/* ==================== BATCH: FILL TOPICS TO 200+ ==================== */
window.DP_INTERVIEW.questions.push(
  {q:"What does map() do?",level:"Functions",difficulty:"Beginner",experience:"0-4 Years",company:["Amazon","Zoho","Infosys"],answer:"map(func, iterable) applies a function to every item, returning a lazy map object. Wrap in list() to see results. Often replaced by comprehensions in Python.",code:`print(list(map(str.upper,["a","b"])))`,output:`['A', 'B']`,tips:"Comprehensions are often more Pythonic than map()."},
  {q:"What does filter() do?",level:"Functions",difficulty:"Beginner",experience:"0-4 Years",company:["Amazon","Zoho"],answer:"filter(func, iterable) keeps only items where func returns True. Returns a lazy iterator.",code:`print(list(filter(lambda x:x%2==0,[1,2,3,4])))`,output:`[2, 4]`,tips:"[x for x in data if cond] is the comprehension equivalent."},
  {q:"What does reduce() do?",level:"Functions",difficulty:"Intermediate",experience:"1-5 Years",company:["Amazon","Adobe"],answer:"functools.reduce applies a function cumulatively to reduce an iterable to a single value (e.g., product of all numbers). Use sparingly — loops are often clearer.",code:`from functools import reduce\nprint(reduce(lambda a,b:a*b,[1,2,3,4]))`,output:`24`,tips:"reduce is in functools in Python 3, not a built-in."},
  {q:"What is functools.lru_cache?",level:"Functions",difficulty:"Advanced",experience:"2-7 Years",company:["Google","Amazon","Netflix"],answer:"lru_cache is a decorator that caches a function's results based on arguments, so repeated calls return instantly. Great for expensive/recursive functions like Fibonacci.",code:`from functools import lru_cache\n@lru_cache\ndef f(n): return n if n<2 else f(n-1)+f(n-2)\nprint(f(20))`,output:`6765`,tips:"Turns exponential recursion into linear time — huge win."},
  {q:"What is partial function application?",level:"Functions",difficulty:"Advanced",experience:"3-7 Years",company:["Google","Meta"],answer:"functools.partial pre-fills some function arguments, creating a new function with fewer parameters. Useful for callbacks and configuration.",code:`from functools import partial\ncube=partial(pow,exp=3) if False else None\ndouble=partial(lambda x,y:x*y,2)\nprint(double(5))`,output:`10`,tips:"Handy for adapting functions to APIs expecting fewer args."},
  {q:"What is the difference between iterator and generator expression?",level:"Advanced Python",difficulty:"Advanced",experience:"2-6 Years",company:["Google","Amazon"],answer:"A generator expression (x for x in data) is a concise way to create a generator (iterator) with lazy evaluation and low memory. Like a comprehension but with () and lazy.",code:`gen=(x*x for x in range(3))\nprint(next(gen))`,output:`0`,tips:"Use () for generators when you don't need the whole list at once."},
  {q:"What is the __call__ method?",level:"Advanced Python",difficulty:"Advanced",experience:"3-7 Years",company:["Google","Meta","Netflix"],answer:"Defining __call__ makes an instance callable like a function: obj(). Used for stateful function objects and decorators implemented as classes.",code:`class Adder:\n    def __call__(self,a,b): return a+b\nprint(Adder()(2,3))`,output:`5`,tips:"Callable objects are used in class-based decorators."},
  {q:"What is the difference between compile-time and runtime in Python?",level:"Advanced Python",difficulty:"Advanced",experience:"3-8 Years",company:["Google","Microsoft"],answer:"Python compiles source to bytecode (compile-time), then the interpreter executes it (runtime). Syntax errors are caught at compile-time; type/logic errors at runtime.",tips:"Explains why some errors appear before any code runs."},
  {q:"What are Python decorators with arguments?",level:"Advanced Python",difficulty:"Expert",experience:"4-10+ Years",company:["Google","Netflix","Uber"],answer:"A decorator that takes arguments needs THREE nested functions: outer takes the args, middle takes the function, inner wraps it. Common for @retry(3) or @route('/path').",tips:"Three levels of nesting — draw it out mentally."},
  {q:"What is the __slots__ attribute?",level:"Advanced Python",difficulty:"Expert",experience:"4-10+ Years",company:["Google","Amazon","Netflix"],answer:"__slots__ restricts an object's attributes to a fixed set, saving memory by avoiding the per-instance __dict__. Useful when creating millions of small objects.",code:`class P:\n    __slots__=["x","y"]\np=P(); p.x=1\nprint(p.x)`,output:`1`,tips:"Memory optimization for large numbers of instances."},
  {q:"What is the difference between concurrency and parallelism?",level:"Advanced Python",difficulty:"Expert",experience:"4-10+ Years",company:["Google","Uber","Netflix"],answer:"Concurrency is dealing with many tasks by switching between them (one at a time, appears simultaneous). Parallelism is actually running tasks at the same time on multiple cores.",tips:"Concurrency=managing many, Parallelism=doing many simultaneously."},
  {q:"What is a metaclass?",level:"Advanced Python",difficulty:"Expert",experience:"5-10+ Years",company:["Google","Meta"],answer:"A metaclass is the 'class of a class' — it controls how classes are created. type is the default metaclass. Used in frameworks (Django ORM, ABCs) for advanced customization. Rarely needed in app code.",tips:"'99% of developers never need metaclasses' — quote Tim Peters."},
  {q:"What is NumPy broadcasting?",level:"Libraries",difficulty:"Advanced",experience:"2-6 Years",company:["Amazon","Adobe","Walmart Global Tech"],answer:"Broadcasting lets NumPy perform operations on arrays of different shapes by automatically stretching the smaller one, without copying data. Enables fast vectorized math.",code:`import numpy as np\nprint(np.array([1,2,3])+10)`,output:`[11 12 13]`,tips:"Broadcasting avoids explicit loops — key to NumPy speed."},
  {q:"How do you handle missing data in Pandas?",level:"Libraries",difficulty:"Intermediate",experience:"1-5 Years",company:["Amazon","Adobe","Walmart Global Tech"],answer:"Use df.dropna() to remove rows/columns with NaN, or df.fillna(value) to replace them. Check with df.isnull().sum().",tips:"Decide dropna vs fillna based on how much data is missing."},
  {q:"What is the requests library used for?",level:"Libraries",difficulty:"Beginner",experience:"1-5 Years",company:["Amazon","PayPal","Startups"],answer:"requests makes HTTP calls simple: requests.get(url), .post(), etc. Returns a response with .json(), .status_code, .text. The standard way to call REST APIs.",code:`# r = requests.get(url)\n# print(r.status_code, r.json())`,output:`# 200 and parsed JSON`,tips:"Always check r.status_code and handle timeouts/errors."},
  {q:"What is BeautifulSoup used for?",level:"Libraries",difficulty:"Intermediate",experience:"1-5 Years",company:["Startups","Amazon"],answer:"BeautifulSoup parses HTML/XML for web scraping. You navigate the parse tree with find(), find_all(), and CSS selectors to extract data.",tips:"Pair with requests to fetch, then BeautifulSoup to parse."},
  {q:"What is FastAPI and why is it popular?",level:"Libraries",difficulty:"Advanced",experience:"2-7 Years",company:["Uber","Netflix","Startups"],answer:"FastAPI is a modern, high-performance web framework using Python type hints for automatic validation and interactive API docs (Swagger). It's async-first and very fast.",tips:"Mention automatic docs and type-hint validation as key wins."},
  {q:"What are database indexes and why use them?",level:"Database",difficulty:"Advanced",experience:"2-7 Years",company:["Amazon","Oracle","PayPal","Uber"],answer:"An index is a data structure (usually B-tree) that speeds up lookups on a column, like a book's index. It makes reads faster but writes slightly slower and uses extra space.",tips:"Index columns used in WHERE/JOIN; don't over-index (slows writes)."},
  {q:"What is a database transaction and ACID?",level:"Database",difficulty:"Advanced",experience:"3-8 Years",company:["Amazon","Oracle","PayPal"],answer:"A transaction is a group of operations that succeed or fail together. ACID = Atomicity, Consistency, Isolation, Durability — the guarantees that keep data reliable.",tips:"Give the bank transfer example: debit + credit must both succeed."},
  {q:"What is the N+1 query problem?",level:"Database",difficulty:"Expert",experience:"3-10+ Years",company:["Amazon","PayPal","Uber"],answer:"N+1 happens when code runs 1 query to get a list, then 1 more query per item (N queries). Fix with eager loading / JOINs (e.g., select_related in Django) to batch into fewer queries.",tips:"A classic ORM performance bug — name the fix (eager loading)."},
  {q:"What are HTTP status codes you should know?",level:"APIs",difficulty:"Beginner",experience:"1-6 Years",company:["Amazon","PayPal","Uber"],answer:"200 OK, 201 Created, 204 No Content, 301 Moved, 400 Bad Request, 401 Unauthorized, 403 Forbidden, 404 Not Found, 500 Server Error. 2xx=success, 4xx=client error, 5xx=server error.",tips:"Know the ranges: 2xx ok, 3xx redirect, 4xx client, 5xx server."},
  {q:"What is OAuth?",level:"APIs",difficulty:"Advanced",experience:"3-8 Years",company:["Google","PayPal","Uber"],answer:"OAuth is an authorization framework that lets apps access resources on a user's behalf WITHOUT sharing passwords (e.g., 'Login with Google'). It issues access tokens with limited scope.",tips:"Note OAuth is authorization; OpenID Connect adds authentication."},
  {q:"What is rate limiting in APIs?",level:"APIs",difficulty:"Advanced",experience:"3-8 Years",company:["Uber","PayPal","Netflix"],answer:"Rate limiting caps how many requests a client can make in a time window to prevent abuse and ensure fair use. Implemented with token bucket or sliding window algorithms; returns 429 Too Many Requests.",tips:"Mention the 429 status code and token-bucket algorithm."},
  {q:"What is the AAA pattern in unit testing?",level:"Testing",difficulty:"Intermediate",experience:"1-6 Years",company:["Google","Amazon","Microsoft"],answer:"Arrange (set up data), Act (call the function), Assert (verify the result). It keeps tests readable and well-structured.",tips:"Structure every test as Arrange-Act-Assert."},
  {q:"What is code coverage?",level:"Testing",difficulty:"Intermediate",experience:"2-6 Years",company:["Google","Amazon"],answer:"Code coverage measures the percentage of code executed by tests (via coverage.py). High coverage helps but doesn't guarantee correctness — test quality matters more than quantity.",tips:"Warn that 100% coverage doesn't mean bug-free."},
  {q:"SCENARIO: How do you make an existing slow API endpoint faster?",level:"Scenario-Based",difficulty:"Expert",experience:"3-10+ Years",company:["Uber","PayPal","Amazon"],answer:"Profile to find the bottleneck. Add caching (Redis), optimize DB queries/indexes, fix N+1 problems, paginate large results, use async for I/O, and add a CDN for static content.",tips:"Structure the answer: measure -> cache -> DB -> async -> scale."},
  {q:"SCENARIO: Your app crashes randomly in production. Where do you start?",level:"Scenario-Based",difficulty:"Expert",experience:"3-10+ Years",company:["Netflix","Amazon","Uber"],answer:"Check error logs and stack traces, monitoring dashboards, and recent deployments. Reproduce with similar inputs. Add alerting. Look for memory issues, unhandled exceptions, or race conditions.",tips:"Start with logs and recent changes — most bugs come from recent deploys."},
  {q:"SCENARIO: How would you process a 50GB file that doesn't fit in memory?",level:"Scenario-Based",difficulty:"Expert",experience:"3-10+ Years",company:["Google","Amazon","Netflix"],answer:"Stream it: read line by line or in chunks (never load it all). Use generators for lazy processing. For heavy work, use chunked processing with pandas (chunksize) or distributed tools like Spark.",tips:"Key word: 'streaming' / generators — never load 50GB into memory."},
  {q:"What is the difference between a shallow bug fix and root cause analysis?",level:"Scenario-Based",difficulty:"Advanced",experience:"3-8 Years",company:["Google","Amazon"],answer:"A shallow fix patches the symptom; root cause analysis (RCA) finds WHY it happened to prevent recurrence. Use the '5 Whys' technique and add a regression test.",tips:"Mention adding a test so the bug can never return."},
  {q:"How do you explain your best project in an interview?",level:"HR & Behavioural",difficulty:"Intermediate",experience:"0-10+ Years",company:["All Companies"],answer:"Structure: problem it solved, your role, tech stack, key challenges + how you solved them, and the impact/result. Keep it focused and be ready for deep technical follow-ups.",tips:"Know EVERY line of your resume projects — expect deep drilling."},
  {q:"How do you handle constructive criticism?",level:"HR & Behavioural",difficulty:"Intermediate",experience:"0-10+ Years",company:["All Companies"],answer:"Listen without defensiveness, ask clarifying questions, thank them, and act on valid points. Show a growth mindset with a real example where feedback improved your work.",tips:"Show you value feedback as a growth tool, not an attack."},
  {q:"Why are you leaving your current job?",level:"HR & Behavioural",difficulty:"Intermediate",experience:"1-10+ Years",company:["All Companies"],answer:"Stay positive — focus on seeking growth, new challenges, or better alignment with your goals. Never badmouth your current employer.",tips:"Never speak negatively about a past/current employer."},
  {q:"How do you prioritize tasks when everything is urgent?",level:"HR & Behavioural",difficulty:"Advanced",experience:"2-10+ Years",company:["Amazon","Google","All Companies"],answer:"Assess impact vs effort, clarify true deadlines with stakeholders, use frameworks like the Eisenhower matrix, and communicate trade-offs transparently.",tips:"Mention communicating trade-offs — shows maturity."},
  {q:"What motivates you as a developer?",level:"HR & Behavioural",difficulty:"Beginner",experience:"0-10+ Years",company:["All Companies"],answer:"Be genuine: solving real problems, learning new tech, seeing your code create impact, and collaborating with great teams. Tie it to the role.",tips:"Authenticity beats a rehearsed 'perfect' answer here."}
);


