window.DP_INTERVIEW.questions.push(
  {q:"What is the difference between 'append' and 'insert' in a list?",level:"Collections",difficulty:"Beginner",experience:"0-3 Years",company:["TCS","Zoho"],answer:"append(x) adds x to the end (O(1)). insert(i, x) adds x at index i, shifting elements right (O(n)).",code:`a=[1,2,3]; a.insert(1,99); print(a)`,output:`[1, 99, 2, 3]`,tips:"append is O(1), insert is O(n) due to shifting."},
  {q:"What is the difference between 'any()' and 'all()'?",level:"Python Basics",difficulty:"Beginner",experience:"0-4 Years",company:["Amazon","Zoho"],answer:"any() returns True if AT LEAST one element is truthy. all() returns True only if ALL elements are truthy (or empty).",code:`print(any([0,0,1]), all([1,1,0]))`,output:`True False`,tips:"Both short-circuit — stop as soon as the answer is known."},
  {q:"How do you flatten a dictionary of lists into pairs?",level:"Coding Problems",difficulty:"Intermediate",experience:"1-5 Years",company:["Amazon","Adobe"],answer:"Iterate items and expand each list with a nested comprehension into (key, value) pairs.",code:`d={"a":[1,2],"b":[3]}\nprint([(k,v) for k,vs in d.items() for v in vs])`,output:`[('a', 1), ('a', 2), ('b', 3)]`,tips:"Nested comprehension order matches nested-loop order."},
  {q:"What is the difference between 'copy' and 'assignment' for lists?",level:"Collections",difficulty:"Intermediate",experience:"1-4 Years",company:["Amazon","Microsoft"],answer:"Assignment (b=a) makes both names point to the SAME list. copy (b=a.copy()) makes an independent list. Modifying an assigned alias affects the original.",code:`a=[1,2]; b=a; b.append(3); print(a)`,output:`[1, 2, 3]`,tips:"Aliasing bugs are common — use .copy() when you need independence."},
  {q:"What is the purpose of if __name__ == '__main__'?",level:"Python Basics",difficulty:"Intermediate",experience:"1-5 Years",company:["Amazon","Google","TCS"],answer:"It ensures code runs only when the file is executed directly, NOT when imported as a module. Lets a file be both a script and an importable module.",code:`if __name__=="__main__":\n    print("run directly")`,output:`run directly`,tips:"Very common question — explain the script vs import distinction."},
  {q:"What is the difference between 'del' and setting a variable to None?",level:"Python Basics",difficulty:"Intermediate",experience:"1-5 Years",company:["Microsoft","Adobe"],answer:"del removes the variable name entirely (accessing it later raises NameError). Setting to None keeps the name but clears its value.",tips:"del removes the reference; None keeps the name alive."},
  {q:"How do you convert a string number to int safely?",level:"Python Basics",difficulty:"Beginner",experience:"0-4 Years",company:["TCS","Zoho","Amazon"],answer:"Wrap int() in try-except to handle non-numeric input gracefully, returning a default on failure.",code:`def to_int(s):\n    try: return int(s)\n    except ValueError: return 0\nprint(to_int("abc"))`,output:`0`,tips:"Always validate/handle user input that may not be numeric."},
  {q:"What is the difference between '/' and '//' operators?",level:"Python Basics",difficulty:"Beginner",experience:"0-3 Years",company:["TCS","Infosys","Zoho"],answer:"/ is true division (always returns float). // is floor division (returns the largest whole number less than or equal to the result).",code:`print(7/2, 7//2, -7//2)`,output:`3.5 3 -4`,tips:"Note -7//2 is -4 (floors toward negative infinity), not -3."},
  {q:"What is a docstring?",level:"Python Basics",difficulty:"Beginner",experience:"0-4 Years",company:["Google","Amazon","TCS"],answer:"A docstring is a string literal right after a function/class/module definition that documents it. Accessible via .__doc__ and used by help(). Use triple quotes.",code:`def f():\n    """Adds numbers."""\n    pass\nprint(f.__doc__)`,output:`Adds numbers.`,tips:"Follow PEP 257 docstring conventions in professional code."},
  {q:"What is the Global Interpreter Lock's impact on I/O tasks?",level:"Advanced Python",difficulty:"Expert",experience:"4-10+ Years",company:["Google","Uber","Netflix"],answer:"For I/O-bound tasks, the GIL is released during I/O waits, so threading DOES help (threads run while others wait for network/disk). The GIL only limits CPU-bound parallelism.",tips:"Clarify: GIL hurts CPU-bound threading, not I/O-bound threading."},
  {q:"What is list vs generator memory-wise?",level:"Advanced Python",difficulty:"Advanced",experience:"2-6 Years",company:["Google","Amazon","Netflix"],answer:"A list stores all elements in memory at once (O(n) space). A generator produces elements one at a time (O(1) space), ideal for large or infinite sequences.",code:`import sys\nprint(sys.getsizeof([x for x in range(1000)]) > sys.getsizeof((x for x in range(1000))))`,output:`True`,tips:"Generators trade re-usability for huge memory savings."}
);


